# CODEATTRIBUTIONS
# Context Bubble — AI Usage Notes
# Author: stice24
# Date: 2026-04-28

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Used Claude Code (Anthropic) as a coding assistant across all 9 sessions of this project. These are notes on where AI actually helped and where I was in the weeds myself.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Where Claude actually helped

**contextBubbleController.ts — symbol extraction**
The multi-strategy symbol resolution (_resolveSymbolFromSelection, strategies 1-4)
was drafted with AI help. The regex for named function keyword, class keyword,
and identifier-before-paren are all in there. I understood what each strategy
was doing and kept them, but I wouldn't have written that brace-counting logic
in _findFunctionBodyRange from scratch quickly. Also the fallback
_fetchCallGraphViaText — the scanning logic for callers/callees via text
pattern matching when LSP isn't available was AI-assisted.

**contextBubbleArrow.ts — bezier curve**
The control point math for the SVG bezier connecting the anchor to the bubble
was drafted by Claude. I knew what I wanted visually (a curve that felt natural
regardless of where you dragged the bubble) but the dynamic control point
calculation was easier to iterate on with AI generating a starting point.

**callGraphSlot.ts — LSP wiring**
Getting the CallHierarchyProvider working, including the fallback chain to
ReferenceProvider + DefinitionProvider, involved a lot of navigating VS Code's
internal provider interfaces. Claude helped me find the right service hooks and
draft the provider resolution. I caught a bug where the fallback was running
even when call hierarchy was available and fixed that myself.

**gitHistorySlot.ts — git service integration**
Claude drafted the initial git service wiring. The decision to scope queries to
file + line range was mine, but finding the right internal service interface in
Code OSS to do that without shelling out directly — Claude helped locate that.
parseMessage() for extracting PR refs from commit messages is fully Claude-generated.

**slackMentionsSlot.ts — Slack API fetch + auth**
The Slack Search API fetch logic and the token retrieval from VS Code settings
was drafted with AI assistance. The channel picker UI, the connect/disconnect
state flow, and the slack:// deep-link behavior — I wrote and iterated those
myself.

**Disposal boilerplate across all files**
The DisposableStore and IDisposable patterns used throughout are heavily informed
by Claude knowing the VS Code service layer. I would have learned it eventually
but it would have taken longer.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## General note

I reviewed every block of generated code before accepting it. Several suggested approaches got rejected outright — early on Claude wanted to use a webview panel
for the bubble, extend existing overlay widgets instead of building custom DOM, and shell out to git directly. All of those got thrown out. The architecture
decisions in CLAUDE.md reflect what I actually wanted to build, and I used that doc to keep the AI on track rather than letting it design the thing.

